<edge source="sub_office:na90:e748b0fc5d" target="mstc_challenge:streamlining_production_removing_red_tape_and_en:5c22e0a6a3" id="funds_challenge_evidence:programming_record_programming_data_csv_52">
  <data key="d11">funds_challenge_evidence</data>
  <data key="d12">1</data>
  <data key="d13">classification_pipeline</data>
  <data key="d14">52</data>
  <data key="d15">medium</data>
  <data key="d16">True</data>
  <data key="d17">The scope description emphasizes “Telecommunication &amp; AI Infrastructure Program Integration” under Infrastructure and Operations / Operations of Facilities, indicating AI-enabled integration of operational infrastructure rather than design, materials R&amp;D, or enterprise business analytics. Among the options, the Streamlining Production challenge most closely aligns with deploying AI and digital infrastructure into high-hazard facility and operations data systems to improve efficiency, compliance, and safety. The focus on operations of facilities and AI infrastructure integration supports this mapping, though the brief scope text leaves some ambiguity, so confidence is medium.</data>
  <data key="d18">UFR</data>
  <data key="d19">15220000.0</data>
  <data key="d20">NA90</data>
</edge>
<edge source="sub_office:na90:e748b0fc5d" target="mstc_challenge:increasing_experimental_capacity_at_nuclear_rese:e9a4a1153b" id="funds_challenge_evidence:programming_record_programming_data_csv_49">
  <data key="d11">funds_challenge_evidence</data>
  <data key="d12">1</data>
  <data key="d13">classification_pipeline</data>
  <data key="d14">49</data>
  <data key="d15">low</data>
  <data key="d16">True</data>
  <data key="d17">The scope describes replacing overloaded telecommunications towers to meet current and future communication demands, categorized under Infrastructure and Operations / Maintenance and Repair of Facilities. This is generic infrastructure support with no direct indication of regulatory streamlining, acquisition, materials, or forensics. Improved communications infrastructure most plausibly supports increased experimental and operational capacity at facilities by enhancing connectivity and throughput, which aligns best—though weakly—with the experimental capacity challenge compared to the more process- and domain-specific alternatives.</data>
  <data key="d18">UFR</data>
  <data key="d19">46400000.0</data>
  <data key="d20">NA90</data>
</edge>
<edge source="sub_office:na90:e748b0fc5d" target="mstc_challenge:increasing_experimental_capacity_at_nuclear_rese:e9a4a1153b" id="funds_challenge_evidence:programming_record_programming_data_csv_50">
  <data key="d11">funds_challenge_evidence</data>
  <data key="d12">1</data>
  <data key="d13">classification_pipeline</data>
  <data key="d14">50</data>
  <data key="d15">medium</data>
  <data key="d16">False</data>
  <data key="d17">The scope focuses on upgrading LANL’s electrical power capacity, including new high-voltage import and distribution lines specifically to the Supercomputing Center and between substations. This directly supports increased power availability for mission work, enabling higher throughput and reliability of supercomputing and experimental facilities. Among the challenges, this aligns best with “Increasing Experimental Capacity at Nuclear Research Facilities,” which emphasizes leveraging supercomputing and facility infrastructure to expand experimental capacity. The other challenges center on acquisition processes, materials workflows, regulatory/safety documentation, or DA/PA integration, none of which are directly addressed by an electrical power capacity upgrade project. SME correction: Corrected</data>
  <data key="d18">BASELINE</data>
  <data key="d19">115104000.0</data>
  <data key="d20">NA90</data>
</edge>
<edge source="sub_office:na90:e748b0fc5d" target="mstc_challenge:increasing_experimental_capacity_at_nuclear_rese:e9a4a1153b" id="funds_challenge_evidence:programming_record_programming_data_csv_53">
  <data key="d11">funds_challenge_evidence</data>
  <data key="d12">1</data>
  <data key="d13">classification_pipeline</data>
  <data key="d14">53</data>
  <data key="d15">medium</data>
  <data key="d16">True</data>
  <data key="d17">The scope description focuses on an Electrical Power Capacity Upgrade at LANL, categorized under Infrastructure and Operations / Recapitalization. Among the challenges, the one most aligned with infrastructure upgrades at nuclear research facilities is “Increasing Experimental Capacity at Nuclear Research Facilities,” which explicitly concerns enhancing capacity and throughput at limited-capacity, high-consequence facilities. Upgrading site electrical power is a foundational infrastructure investment that enables higher experimental load, additional equipment, and more reliable operations, indirectly supporting increased experimental capacity. Other challenges center on acquisition, business processes, materials R&amp;D workflows, or nuclear forensics, which are less consistent with a power-capacity infrastructure project.</data>
  <data key="d18">BASELINE</data>
  <data key="d19">2824000.0</data>
  <data key="d20">NA90</data>
</edge>
<edge source="sub_office:na90:e748b0fc5d" target="mstc_challenge:increasing_experimental_capacity_at_nuclear_rese:e9a4a1153b" id="funds_challenge_evidence:programming_record_programming_data_csv_54">
  <data key="d11">funds_challenge_evidence</data>
  <data key="d12">1</data>
  <data key="d13">classification_pipeline</data>
  <data key="d14">54</data>
  <data key="d15">low</data>
  <data key="d16">False</data>
  <data key="d17">The scope description only indicates funding for a new central computing facility at Y-12, which most directly supports computational and experimental capacity rather than materials development, acquisition, or business-process platforms. Among the options, expanding computing infrastructure aligns best with increasing experimental capacity (through more simulation, data processing, and facility support). However, the evidence is minimal and generic, so confidence is low. SME correction: Corrected</data>
  <data key="d18">BASELINE</data>
  <data key="d19">3399000.0</data>
  <data key="d20">NA90</data>
</edge>
<edge source="sub_office:na90:e748b0fc5d" target="mstc_challenge:harnessing_america_s_historic_nuclear_data_and_r:6cbae10ba9" id="funds_challenge_evidence:programming_record_programming_data_csv_51">
  <data key="d11">funds_challenge_evidence</data>
  <data key="d12">1</data>
  <data key="d13">classification_pipeline</data>
  <data key="d14">51</data>
  <data key="d15">medium</data>
  <data key="d16">True</data>
  <data key="d17">The scope is to ensure reliable classified and unclassified computing availability by constructing a central computing facility with space for network and data servers/hubs and personnel. This directly supports secure data storage, processing, and access for both classified and unclassified information, aligning most closely with the challenge focused on harnessing and managing America’s historic nuclear data and research. While the description does not explicitly mention digitization workflows, it clearly centers on data and computing infrastructure rather than materials, design/production integration, experimental facilities, or acquisition processes, making the data-focused challenge the best fit.</data>
  <data key="d18">BASELINE</data>
  <data key="d19">183700000.0</data>
  <data key="d20">NA90</data>
</edge>
<edge source="sub_office:na90:e748b0fc5d" target="mstc_challenge:accelerating_business_and_management_processes_f:1739dbeef6" id="funds_challenge_evidence:programming_record_programming_data_fynsp27_wide_csv_56">
  <data key="d11">funds_challenge_evidence</data>
  <data key="d12">1</data>
  <data key="d13">classification_pipeline</data>
  <data key="d14">56</data>
  <data key="d15">medium</data>
  <data key="d16">True</data>
  <data key="d17">The scope focuses on an enterprise AI tool to inform NNSA infrastructure decision making and help NNSA “Build SMART with confidence,” which is fundamentally about improving planning and decision processes for facilities and infrastructure. This aligns best with the business and management challenge that emphasizes unified data, AI-enabled decision support, and improved planning and execution across management functions, rather than with production, materials, or design/production integration challenges. The WBS and STAT codes for Maintenance and Repair of Facilities further support that this is an infrastructure planning and management use case, not acquisition-specific or manufacturing-focused.</data>
  <data key="d18">UFR</data>
  <data key="d19">32950000.0</data>
  <data key="d20">NA90</data>
</edge>
