<edge source="programming_record:programming_data_fynsp27_wide_csv:32" target="mstc_challenge:integrating_design_and_production_operations_for:a1b61701e5" id="classified_as_challenge:programming_record_programming_data_fynsp27_wide_csv_32">
  <data key="d11">classified_as_challenge</data>
  <data key="d12">1</data>
  <data key="d13">classification_pipeline</data>
  <data key="d14">32</data>
  <data key="d15">low</data>
  <data key="d16">False</data>
  <data key="d17">The scope description and program request only state "Developing Artificial Intelligence Tools for Nuclear Deterrence" with no further detail. This clearly links to deterrence but does not specify whether the AI tools are for design/production integration, forensics attribution, emergency response, nonproliferation control, or acquisition. Given the STAT/WBS context (Assessment Science, Dynamic Materials Properties) and the explicit deterrence framing, the most plausible fit is a deterrence-focused operational capability rather than acquisition or enterprise processes. Among the deterrence-oriented challenges, attribution of nuclear and radiological signatures is the closest match to generic AI tools supporting deterrence and assessment, but the evidence is sparse and non-specific, so confidence is low. SME correction: Corrected</data>
  <data key="d18">ROT</data>
  <data key="d19">115239000.0</data>
  <data key="d20">NA11</data>
</edge>
