  <data key="d15">medium</data>
  <data key="d16">True</data>
  <data key="d17">The scope description and program request both emphasize “Developing Artificial Intelligence Tools for Nuclear Deterrence,” which aligns most directly with AI applied to the nuclear weapons enterprise itself rather than to forensics, emergency response, or acquisition. Among the options, the Integrating Design and Production Operations for Nuclear Deterrence challenge is specifically focused on AI-enabled tools that support deterrence by improving nuclear weapons design/production processes. The STAT context (Stockpile Research, Technology, and Engineering; Assessment Science; Hydrodynamic &amp; Subcritical Execution Support) also points to core stockpile and design/assessment activities, reinforcing this mapping over attribution, emergency response, acquisition, or general experimental facility capacity. Because the scope text is brief and could, in principle, map to other deterrence-related AI efforts, confidence is medium rather than high.</data>
  <data key="d18">ROT</data>
  <data key="d19">13576000.0</data>
  <data key="d20">NA11</data>
</edge>
