  <data key="d16">False</data>
  <data key="d17">The scope description and program request both emphasize “Developing Artificial Intelligence Tools for Nuclear Deterrence,” which is broad but clearly tied to deterrence and AI. Among the challenges, the one most directly focused on nuclear deterrence in a core stockpile/weapon context is Integrating Design and Production Operations for Nuclear Deterrence. The STAT lineage (Stockpile Research, Technology, and Engineering; Assessment Science; Hydrodynamic &amp; Subcritical Execution Support) indicates work closely associated with weapons design, assessment, and execution support rather than emergency response, forensics, enterprise acquisition, or generic facility operations. While the description does not explicitly mention design–production integration, its deterrence focus within a stockpile R&amp;D/assessment context aligns best with the design/production modernization mission, so that challenge is the closest fit. SME correction: Corrected</data>
