  <data key="d15">low</data>
  <data key="d16">False</data>
  <data key="d17">The scope description is extremely short and generic: “AI For Deterrence,” and the program request is “DEVELOPING ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE TOOLS FOR NUCLEAR DETERRENCE.” This only indicates AI applied to deterrence, without specifying design/production integration, materials, emergency response, regulatory streamlining, or forensic attribution. Among the candidate challenges, the one whose core purpose most directly and explicitly ties AI to nuclear deterrence itself (rather than to production, materials, or internal processes) is the attribution-focused challenge, which aims to strengthen deterrence by ensuring adversaries can be held accountable. However, because the evidence does not mention signatures, forensics, response, production, or materials explicitly, the mapping is highly ambiguous and confidence is low. SME correction: Corrected</data>
  <data key="d18">ROT</data>
  <data key="d19">141431000.0</data>
  <data key="d20">NA11</data>
</edge>
