<edge source="programming_record:programming_data_fynsp27_wide_csv:43" target="mstc_challenge:integrating_design_and_production_operations_for:a1b61701e5" id="classified_as_challenge:programming_record_programming_data_fynsp27_wide_csv_43">
  <data key="d11">classified_as_challenge</data>
  <data key="d12">1</data>
  <data key="d13">classification_pipeline</data>
  <data key="d14">43</data>
  <data key="d15">medium</data>
  <data key="d16">False</data>
  <data key="d17">The scope description and program request both state “Developing Artificial Intelligence Tools for Nuclear Deterrence,” which is broad but clearly tied to deterrence and AI in support of the stockpile (NA-11, Stockpile Research, Technology, and Engineering; HED &amp; Ignition Science for Stockpile Applications). Among the candidate challenges, the one most directly focused on nuclear deterrence within the weapons enterprise is “Integrating Design and Production Operations for Nuclear Deterrence,” which targets AI-enabled capabilities to support nuclear weapons design, production, and modernization. Other options emphasize forensics, emergency response, historic data, or acquisition rather than core deterrent design/stockpile functions. Because the evidence does not specify design/production integration explicitly, but does clearly emphasize deterrence in a stockpile science context, the mapping is appropriate but not certain, leading to medium confidence.</data>
  <data key="d18">ROT</data>
  <data key="d19">24365000.0</data>
  <data key="d20">NA11</data>
</edge>
