<edge source="sub_office:na20:3f63a5531c" target="mstc_challenge:safeguarding_nuclear_materials_from_proliferatio:616a01e1a8" id="funds_challenge_evidence:programming_record_programming_data_csv_35">
  <data key="d11">funds_challenge_evidence</data>
  <data key="d12">1</data>
  <data key="d13">classification_pipeline</data>
  <data key="d14">35</data>
  <data key="d15">high</data>
  <data key="d16">False</data>
  <data key="d17">The scope description focuses on strengthening international law enforcement, intelligence, and security agencies’ capabilities to detect, disrupt, and investigate nuclear smuggling. This aligns directly with nonproliferation and preventing illicit movement of nuclear materials, which is the core of the Safeguarding Nuclear Materials from Proliferation Threats challenge. The WBS and STAT hierarchy (Global Material Security, Nuclear Smuggling Detection and Deterrence) further support that the mission is about detecting and deterring proliferation-related smuggling rather than emergency response, forensics, enterprise acquisition, or weapons design/production.</data>
  <data key="d18">UFR</data>
  <data key="d19">79705000.0</data>
  <data key="d20">NA20</data>
</edge>
<edge source="sub_office:na20:3f63a5531c" target="mstc_challenge:safeguarding_nuclear_materials_from_proliferatio:616a01e1a8" id="funds_challenge_evidence:programming_record_programming_data_csv_37">
  <data key="d11">funds_challenge_evidence</data>
  <data key="d12">1</data>
  <data key="d13">classification_pipeline</data>
  <data key="d14">37</data>
  <data key="d15">medium</data>
  <data key="d16">True</data>
  <data key="d17">The scope description is generic (“AI PROGRAM WORK”), so supporting fields drive the mapping. The STAT hierarchy and WBS are both explicitly “Proliferation Detection” under Defense Nuclear Nonproliferation R&amp;D (NA-20), which aligns directly with AI-enabled detection of nuclear proliferation activities. Among the candidate challenges, “Safeguarding Nuclear Materials from Proliferation Threats” is specifically focused on nonproliferation and detection of proliferation-relevant anomalies, matching the proliferation detection mission area indicated by the programming evidence.</data>
  <data key="d18">BASELINE</data>
  <data key="d19">165000000.0</data>
  <data key="d20">NA20</data>
</edge>
<edge source="sub_office:na20:3f63a5531c" target="mstc_challenge:safeguarding_nuclear_materials_from_proliferatio:616a01e1a8" id="funds_challenge_evidence:programming_record_programming_data_csv_40">
  <data key="d11">funds_challenge_evidence</data>
  <data key="d12">1</data>
  <data key="d13">classification_pipeline</data>
  <data key="d14">40</data>
  <data key="d15">high</data>
  <data key="d16">True</data>
  <data key="d17">The scope focuses on supporting and implementing activities under EO 14299 and expanding IAEA safeguards at U.S. nuclear facilities, which is directly tied to international safeguards, nonproliferation, and monitoring of nuclear materials. The WBS and STAT lines are for Civil Nuclear Cooperation &amp; Engagement under Nonproliferation and Arms Control (NA-20), reinforcing that the effort is about safeguards and nonproliferation rather than emergency response, acquisition, or experimental capacity. This aligns most closely with the Safeguarding Nuclear Materials from Proliferation Threats challenge, which centers on enhancing monitoring, detection, and enforcement of nonproliferation commitments. The (CUI) AI-induced proliferation challenge lacks specific safeguards/IAEA context in the provided description, making it a weaker fit than the safeguards-focused challenge.</data>
  <data key="d18">UFR</data>
  <data key="d19">20000000.0</data>
  <data key="d20">NA20</data>
</edge>
<edge source="sub_office:na20:3f63a5531c" target="mstc_challenge:safeguarding_nuclear_materials_from_proliferatio:616a01e1a8" id="funds_challenge_evidence:programming_record_programming_data_csv_41">
  <data key="d11">funds_challenge_evidence</data>
  <data key="d12">1</data>
  <data key="d13">classification_pipeline</data>
  <data key="d14">41</data>
  <data key="d15">high</data>
  <data key="d16">True</data>
  <data key="d17">The scope focuses on upgrading export control IT systems to accelerate technical reviews of export licenses for U.S. nuclear and dual-use commodities. This directly supports nonproliferation and arms control by improving oversight of nuclear-related exports and preventing diversion of sensitive materials and technologies to proliferation threats. The STAT and WBS context (Nonproliferation and Arms Control, Nuclear Export Controls, NA-20) further align with the Safeguarding Nuclear Materials from Proliferation Threats challenge, which centers on enhancing monitoring, analysis, and enforcement to prevent nuclear proliferation.</data>
  <data key="d18">UFR</data>
  <data key="d19">8000000.0</data>
  <data key="d20">NA20</data>
</edge>
<edge source="sub_office:na20:3f63a5531c" target="mstc_challenge:strengthening_deterrence_through_attribution_of:12318fe125" id="partners_on_challenge">
  <data key="d11">partners_on_challenge</data>
  <data key="d12">1</data>
  <data key="d13">ProgramOffice_Mapping.csv</data>
  <data key="d14">7</data>
</edge>
<edge source="sub_office:na20:3f63a5531c" target="mstc_challenge:strengthening_deterrence_through_attribution_of:12318fe125" id="funds_challenge_evidence:programming_record_programming_data_csv_36">
  <data key="d11">funds_challenge_evidence</data>
  <data key="d12">1</data>
  <data key="d13">classification_pipeline</data>
  <data key="d14">36</data>
  <data key="d15">high</data>
  <data key="d16">True</data>
  <data key="d17">The scope description explicitly focuses on significantly accelerating nuclear forensics attribution timelines for nuclear or radiological incidents and leveraging AI for post-event processing. This directly aligns with the attribution-focused Genesis challenge, which targets AI-enabled nuclear forensics to rapidly determine provenance of nuclear and radiological materials. Supporting evidence includes the STAT and WBS labels for Forensics R&amp;D and the program request title “AI ACCELERATION FOR NUCLEAR FORENSICS TIMELINES,” all of which point to nuclear forensics attribution rather than broader threat assessment, acquisition, nonproliferation monitoring, or historic data digitization.</data>
  <data key="d18">UFR</data>
  <data key="d19">150000000.0</data>
  <data key="d20">NA20</data>
</edge>
<edge source="sub_office:na20:3f63a5531c" target="mstc_challenge:strengthening_deterrence_through_attribution_of:12318fe125" id="funds_challenge_evidence:programming_record_programming_data_fynsp27_wide_csv_30">
  <data key="d11">funds_challenge_evidence</data>
  <data key="d12">1</data>
  <data key="d13">classification_pipeline</data>
  <data key="d14">30</data>
  <data key="d15">high</data>
  <data key="d16">False</data>
  <data key="d17">The scope focuses on developing technologies to detect and characterize foreign nuclear weapons programs and to monitor, characterize, and interdict nuclear materials outside regulatory control. This aligns directly with nonproliferation monitoring, anomaly detection, and safeguarding nuclear materials from rogue actors, which is the core of the “Safeguarding Nuclear Materials from Proliferation Threats” challenge. Supporting evidence includes STAT lines under Defense Nuclear Nonproliferation R&amp;D and Proliferation Detection, reinforcing the nonproliferation detection mission rather than emergency response, acquisition, or deterrence production. SME correction: Corrected</data>
  <data key="d18">BASELINE</data>
  <data key="d19">165000000.0</data>
  <data key="d20">NA20</data>
</edge>
